  cites Poc chapter Spike

 SPIKE ONLY. THROWAWAY. How a still frame goes out as text, for
 harness/spike_svg.py to turn into a picture. Nothing grades this.

 A CHAPTER OF ITS OWN BECAUSE THERE ARE TWO ENTRIES NOW, and one of them cannot
 simply cite the other: a bundle is ONE FLAT NAMESPACE (PORTING_NOTES B5), so
 SpikeTruckMain citing SpikeMain for `all-cmds` collided on `opening` --
 "CDX3001 Duplicate definition: opening" -- which is the namespace rule doing
 exactly what it says. The printing moves here, both entries cite it, and neither
 restates it.

 COORDINATES GO OUT AS THEIR EXACT BIT PATTERNS. `show` on a Real is still refused
 by the zig plug -- it needs a __real_to_text it does not have -- so a Real reaches
 the page as an Integer either way. The question is only WHICH integer, and the
 answer stopped being a scale on 2026-08-30.

 THE SCALE WAS INHERITED FROM A DRAWING AND WAS SETTING THE RESOLUTION OF A PROOF.
 These lines were written for harness/spike_svg.py, which paints at 960 by 600,
 where a hundredth of a pixel is already invisible; a hundredth was therefore
 generous. Then `harness/metal.py --entry` began diffing the same text against a
 SECOND COMPILER, and there the last digit printed is the entire resolution of the
 comparison -- two emitters could have disagreed by four orders of magnitude and
 the run would have said AGREES. Millionths bought four orders. `real-to-bits`
 ends the argument instead of moving it: the pattern IS the value, so the diff is
 exact and there is no last digit to be generous with.

 WHAT THAT MAKES THE THIRD ARM ABLE TO SEE, which it could not before: FMA
 contraction, x87 excess precision, a reassociated sum -- every emitter fault that
 lives at 1e-16 and was invisible under any decimal scale (PORTING_NOTES D11 is
 where that limit was measured and admitted). It also makes the SVG exact rather
 than rounded, which costs nothing and was never the point.

 A pattern is a signed i64, so roughly half of them print with a minus sign and
 none of them are readable. That is the trade: this text stopped being for a human
 the day a second compiler started reading it.

 ONE LINE FOR THE WHOLE FRAME, commands separated by semicolons, rather than a
 print per command. A Codex `act` has no recursive loop shape, and a chapter is
 not the place to discover one for a throwaway: building the text purely and
 handing it to a single print is the boring option and it is fine here, because a
 frame is about a hundred kilobytes of digits.

   C <tag> <colour> <strength e-3> <n> <x bits> <y bits> ... [<colour2> <geom bits>...]

 We say:

Section: Printing

  scaled : Real -> Text
  scaled (v) = show (real-to-bits v)

  coords : List Real, Integer -> Text
  coords (ps) (i) = coords-range ps i (list-length ps)

 CONCATENATE BY HALVES, NOT ONE AT A TIME, and this is the difference between a
 spike that fits and one that does not. Text is immutable, so `a & rest` allocates
 the whole of `a & rest`; walking a list left to right therefore allocates the sum
 of its own suffixes, which is QUADRATIC in the length. A 2,705-command frame at
 four hundred characters a command is about 1.5 GB of intermediate text to produce
 one megabyte of answer. Splitting the range in half instead copies each byte once
 per LEVEL, so the total is n log n: the same frame costs about 24 MB.

 `text-concat-list` WOULD BE THE IDIOMATIC FORM and it existed the whole time --
 Types/Builtins.codex:213, emitted by this very plug, used by the wasm, C#, python
 and riscv emitters upstream. It copies each byte once where halving copies it once
 per level. The halving stays because it is measured, byte-identical and already
 here; the primitive is the right starting point for the next printer. C17.

 IT WAS BLAMED ON C6 FOR A WEEK. The bump heap never reclaiming is true and is why
 the peak is the total, but the peak was quadratic for a reason that had nothing to
 do with the allocator, and splitting entries was treating the symptom. Measured on
 2026-08-30: spike_cat_main peaked at 3.92 GB of the 4 GiB reserve before this
 change and 108 MB after it, for byte-identical output.

  coords-range : List Real, Integer, Integer -> Text
  coords-range (ps) (lo) (hi) =
    if hi <= lo then ""
    else if hi - lo == 1 then " " & scaled (list-at ps lo)
    else let mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2
      in coords-range ps lo mid & coords-range ps mid hi

 THE GRADIENT TAGS TRAIL THEIR EXTRAS, so a tag 0 or 1 line is byte-identical to
 what this printed before them and the reader needs no new rule for the common
 case. A tag 4, 5 or 6 line carries its second colour and then its own geometry
 AFTER the polygon: the reader knows from the tag how many numbers to expect and
 the point count already told it where the polygon ended.

  extras : DrawCmd -> Text
  extras (c) =
    if c.tag >= 4 then " " & show c.color2 & coords c.geom 0 else ""

  cmd-line : DrawCmd -> Text
  cmd-line (c) =
    "C " & show c.tag & " " & show c.color & " " & show (real-to-int (c.strength * 1000.0)) & " " & show (list-length c.pts / 2) & coords c.pts 0 & extras c

  all-cmds : List DrawCmd, Integer -> Text
  all-cmds (cs) (i) = cmds-range cs i (list-length cs)

  cmds-range : List DrawCmd, Integer, Integer -> Text
  cmds-range (cs) (lo) (hi) =
    if hi <= lo then ""
    else if hi - lo == 1 then cmd-line (list-at cs lo) & " ; "
    else let mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2
      in cmds-range cs lo mid & cmds-range cs mid hi

 One SCENE header per viewpoint, and it carries the sky and horizon colours too,
 because blitter.js paints those itself from the module's numbers and an SVG has
 to do the same or the frame floats on nothing.

  scene-line : Text, Real -> Text
  scene-line (name) (u) =
    "SCENE " & name & " " & show (sky-color (scene-step-at u)) & " " & show (horizon-color (scene-step-at u))
